 "hook_y": 280,
 "badge_line": 4,
 "comment": "Do you think we'd see the next one coming?",
 "youtube": {
  "title": "80 million trees fell, and there was no crater",
  "description": "On the morning of June 30, 1908, a fireball streaked over a remote part of Siberia and exploded in the air near the Tunguska River. The space rock, most likely an asteroid, blew apart about 3 to 6 miles (5 to 10 km) above the ground, flattening an estimated 80 million trees across about 830 square miles (2,150 sq km) of forest, an area bigger than New York City. About 40 miles from the blast, an eyewitness felt a wave of heat \"as if my shirt had caught fire.\" For nights afterward the skies over Europe and Asia glowed, and Londoners could read newsprint at midnight without lamps. The first scientific expedition, led by Leonid Kulik, did not reach the site until 1927; Kulik went looking for a meteorite crater, but there was none.\n\nSources: NASA, \"115 Years Ago: The Tunguska Asteroid Impact Event\"; Royal Museums Greenwich, \"The Tunguska event\"; APS News, \"June 30, 1908: The Tunguska Event\" (2018); Scientific American, \"The Tunguska Mystery: 100 Years Later\" (2008); Discover Magazine, \"The Tunguska Event: A Century Later, It's Still Mysterious\" (2008); EarthSky, \"The Tunguska explosion\"; Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Tunguska event\" and \"New York City\".\nImages: public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. Fallen trees photographed by Leonid Kulik's expedition to the Tunguska site; the Tunguska marshes, photo from Vokrug Sveta magazine (1931), photographer unknown. Maps drawn with Natural Earth data (public domain); the circles show the size of the flattened area and the 40-mile distance, not the real shape of the blast zone.\n\n#history #shorts #tunguska",
  "tags": [
   "history",
   "tunguska event",
   "siberia",
   "asteroid",
   "1908",
   "meteor",
   "space",
   "russia",
   "maps",
   "true story"
  ],
  "category": "EDUCATION"
 },
 "sources": [
  "https://www.nasa.gov/history/115-years-ago-the-tunguska-asteroid-impact-event/",
  "https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/space-astronomy/tunguska-event",
  "https://aps.org/apsnews/2018/06/tunguska-event",
  "https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/the-tunguska-mystery-100-years-later/",
  "https://www.discovermagazine.com/the-tunguska-event-a-century-later-its-still-mysterious-15914",
  "https://earthsky.org/space/what-is-the-tunguska-explosion/",
  "https://www.britannica.com/event/Tunguska-event",
  "https://www.britannica.com/place/New-York-City"
 ],
 "translation_es": "Se cayeron 80 millones de árboles, y no había ningún cráter. En 1908, una bola de fuego gigante cruzó el cielo de Siberia. A 65 kilómetros, un hombre sintió como si se le prendiera fuego la camisa. La explosión aplastó un bosque más grande que la ciudad de Nueva York. En Londres, la gente podía leer el diario a medianoche sin lámparas. En 1927 llegó por fin la primera expedición, buscando un cráter. Pero la roca espacial había explotado a kilómetros de altura, así que… se cayeron 80 millones de árboles, y no había ningún cráter."
}